WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.410
WOLTERSTORFF: Ken is up there. So what I heard

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.119
Roherty saying--

00:00:18.119 --> 00:00:20.090
I knew Roherty, so--

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:22.309
we were friends.

00:00:22.309 --> 00:00:25.249
He-- what he wanted to do was enforce
a Deweyan--

00:00:25.249 --> 00:00:27.279
a pretty thick 

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:30.480
John Deweyan view of democracy on people, and
that just felt

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.390
oppressive to me. 

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:39.790
But you raise the really important question,
of course, of--

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:41.549
can we can eliminate all--

00:00:41.549 --> 00:00:43.530
everything of that sort?

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:47.830
Won't there be a moral gap-- which in a way goes
back to the fear of all these people,

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:48.369
I mean--

00:00:48.369 --> 00:00:49.490
"What do you mean, 

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:51.230
a state which is not,

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.640
unified by some--

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:58.050
which does not have some sort of religious
moral unity in the people?"

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:01.950
Now. So, liberal democracy is clearly not--
it's clearly not a neutral

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.600
arrangement. I mean, it's obviously got
commitments. I think it's got deep commitments

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.020
to human rights, and if people--

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.119
if somebody thinks that there are no human
rights, then they've got real trouble with the

00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:12.190
liberal democracy. 

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:16.850
So-- so I said-- the accusations that
it's amoral-- I don't believe that. In fact

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:18.830
it is

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.160
a moral structure of its own sort, very different

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.450
from previous ones, but it's not

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:27.230
an amoral structure. But I think

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:30.220
on lots of issues that we debate,

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.540
we live with the reality that

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:33.490
we don't have

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:34.780
a shared--

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.299
I mean, so, it's shared structural principles--

00:01:37.299 --> 00:01:42.280
but we live with the reality that we don't
have shared principles which are going to

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.190
yield consensus. 

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:45.649
The present health care

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:50.450
debate-- it is messy because it is filled with
so many economic complications and so forth.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.630
I think the cleanest illustration of this
point was the

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.270
debate over

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:59.660
physician-assisted suicide, which took place
in the state of Oregon about fifteen years ago.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.630
Economics was not--

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:05.100
well, I mean a few people raised an economic
issue-- "it's just too expensive to take care of

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.440
all the people"-- but by and large, that...

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.469
And what you found was,

00:02:09.469 --> 00:02:12.409
a wholeÉ [Inaudible joke from Blair]

00:02:12.409 --> 00:02:16.550
And what you found was a whole array of

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:21.960
fascinatingly articulate but different
moral positions coming out. Not one, but--

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.310
not one secular and not one religious one.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:28.410
There was no religious person who was
so heavy-handed as to say, "God told me last

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:29.509
night that

00:02:29.509 --> 00:02:33.419
physician-assisted suicide is wrong, so it is, 
and that's the end of the matter." Nobody

00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:37.379
was so flat-footed as that. You had utilitarians,
and you had these economizers who said, "It's

00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:42.599
just too expensive to take care of old people and
so forth."

00:02:42.599 --> 00:02:45.119
What we have-- what we do at the end of the
day:

00:02:45.119 --> 00:02:47.119
just take a vote. 

00:02:47.119 --> 00:02:49.479
We haven't achieved consensus.

00:02:49.479 --> 00:02:53.839
We take a vote. And that's a mighty risky procedure.

00:02:53.839 --> 00:02:57.189
You know of a better one? 

00:02:57.189 --> 00:02:57.550
STUDENT: No, I think that's

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:00.679
right. My question--
WOLTERSTORFF: What's the option?

00:03:00.679 --> 00:03:03.429
So, I'm just going to force you to--
STUDENT: My only concern is that--

00:03:03.429 --> 00:03:04.819
how do we ensure that 

00:03:04.819 --> 00:03:08.529
people respect there being a vote, for instance? Is that 
something we just assume,

00:03:08.529 --> 00:03:12.219
that people will always have--"Oh, let's go to a vote." 
That seems to me something that

00:03:12.219 --> 00:03:15.260
does require a particular cultivation of thought-- and I'm concerned

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:19.920
that you saying that we shouldn't traffic in the business
of comprehensive doctrines

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.019
runs the risk of us getting to the point where the vote
is not what we in fact go back to.

00:03:25.019 --> 00:03:30.430
WOLTERSTORFF: So, I think we have to pay a lot of attention
to the character of the preliminary debate.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:37.430
Things that Tony said, things that I said.
A lot of attention to that.

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:39.629
Then-- and one of things-- one of the things--

00:03:39.629 --> 00:03:42.390
one of the reasons that each of us
should listen to the

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:44.469
voice of the other,

00:03:44.469 --> 00:03:48.119
is so that when we decide to vote against
them,

00:03:48.119 --> 00:03:48.699
finally--

00:03:48.699 --> 00:03:49.700
against their position--

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:53.849
we know what we're voting against. We're voting
against Joel, who

00:03:53.849 --> 00:03:57.699
has expressed with great eloquence his own-- whatever. 

00:03:57.699 --> 00:03:59.519
Muslim, Jewish, Buddhist

00:03:59.519 --> 00:04:00.819
position, whatever.

00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:05.409
And now I find that I still disagree with him,
and I have to vote against him.

00:04:05.409 --> 00:04:09.179
I think that's one of the reasons why it's
important to allow these voices to come forth--

00:04:09.179 --> 00:04:10.509
so we know--

00:04:10.509 --> 00:04:11.319
so I--

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:14.269
if I'm voting against Joel, I know

00:04:14.269 --> 00:04:15.709
who it is-- 

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:18.639
what position it is that I'm actually voting against.

00:04:18.639 --> 00:04:20.780
But then we have to take--

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:28.969
take the vote, and try to make that vote
as fair as possible.

00:04:28.969 --> 00:04:30.680
So here-- so here's what's--

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.709
here's what's eating--

00:04:32.709 --> 00:04:37.419
here's what's guiding people like Audi and Rawls
and so forth:

00:04:37.419 --> 00:04:40.409
a noble vision

00:04:40.409 --> 00:04:44.839
of a society in which all of us are co-authors
of the legislation.

00:04:44.839 --> 00:04:46.479
We are co-authors.

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:49.509
Nobody's handing it down; we are co-authors.

00:04:49.509 --> 00:04:51.000
That's the basic idea.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.059
And if you're a co-author, you're not coerced, right?
Of course.

00:04:55.059 --> 00:04:58.189
I think that's a noble idea.

00:04:58.189 --> 00:05:03.439
It just doesn't work. 

00:05:03.439 --> 00:05:05.099
VOLF: É if you need to nurture

00:05:05.099 --> 00:05:12.099
procedures, within those democratic procedures, there are values
and virtues that--
WOLTERSTORFF: Absolutely. 
VOLF: So there are substantive

00:05:13.229 --> 00:05:17.809
virtues embedded in the very democratic--
WOLTERSTORFF: Exactly. We've gotta 

00:05:17.809 --> 00:05:22.259
talk about the virtues of the process
and the debate. Absolutely. 

00:05:22.259 --> 00:05:23.800
My lament is that we're 

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.110
violating those virtues

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:31.459
flamboyantly in the US today.
BLAIR: I mean, I--

00:05:31.459 --> 00:05:33.299
I think it's--

00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:39.080
so I was thinking about

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:42.490
debates, and of the vote and so on, and then
the various

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:45.440
views that we put for, and there was a--

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:51.049
When you lead a political party, basically, 
you are the recipient of a lot of advice,

00:05:51.049 --> 00:05:56.800
and-- of varying degrees of sense--

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:57.909
and...

00:05:57.909 --> 00:06:02.789
the first leader of the British Labour Party
was a Scotch guy named Keir Hardie--

00:06:02.789 --> 00:06:04.969
who was a very tough-minded guy, but he was
trying to get the 

00:06:04.969 --> 00:06:11.029
party off the ground-- win support in an
election for women's suffrage.

00:06:11.029 --> 00:06:11.900
And 

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:15.509
the Pankhursts, who were campaigning for
women's suffrage, they'd also--

00:06:15.509 --> 00:06:17.129
another part of their work was 

00:06:17.129 --> 00:06:19.079
they studied the

00:06:19.079 --> 00:06:20.979
incidence of various

00:06:20.979 --> 00:06:23.330
sexual diseases and they'd

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:28.580
come to the conclusion that this was all because 
of the proclivities of men.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:33.240
And they wrote-- the sisters wrote to 
Keir Hardie, who was the leader, and said--

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.719
before the election-- they said, "We would like to put forth
the following proposition to you:

00:06:37.719 --> 00:06:38.960
that you campaign

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:40.270
on a--

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:41.869
a slogan

00:06:41.869 --> 00:06:48.869
of "Votes for women, and abstinence for men." 
To which Keir Hardie replied-- he said, "Thank you very

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:58.080
much for your advice,

00:06:50.419 --> 00:06:58.080
the election-winning propensities of which
are not discernible."	

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:01.870
So-- don't know why I mentioned that-- but anyway...

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:02.960
I think--

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.529
you see, I think that the-- I think

00:07:05.529 --> 00:07:06.830
that

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:10.249
Nick made a very important
point about the-- 

00:07:10.249 --> 00:07:11.909
the preliminary debate

00:07:11.909 --> 00:07:13.669
at least has got to be

00:07:13.669 --> 00:07:14.889
drawn in a very

00:07:14.889 --> 00:07:17.089
careful way. You see--

00:07:17.089 --> 00:07:22.499
I mean I've faced-- I mean Nathan was asking me for
practical examples-- I mean, just to give a couple:

00:07:22.499 --> 00:07:24.789
we had a-- I started a 

00:07:24.789 --> 00:07:26.870
program of schools in the UK

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:30.410
slightly similar to your charter schools--
but they were called

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:33.289
the academy system, 

00:07:33.289 --> 00:07:36.319
which has now, you know, developed into a major part
of our secondary school systemÉ But

00:07:36.319 --> 00:07:37.869
there were-- 

00:07:37.869 --> 00:07:42.449
there was a major philanthropist
who wanted to start

00:07:42.449 --> 00:07:46.840
such schools up in the-- up in the northeast,
who was a creationist. 

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:52.990
And the question arose whether as a
creationist it was right that he could

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:55.139
start these schools because of the

00:07:55.139 --> 00:07:56.459
influence on-- on

00:07:56.459 --> 00:07:58.009
the children, and in the end, you know,

00:07:58.009 --> 00:07:59.440
sometimes

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:02.749
at the cusp of these things, you have to decide
it in quite a difficult way,

00:08:02.749 --> 00:08:04.010
so we decided in the end--

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:04.969
I decided--

00:08:04.969 --> 00:08:06.030
he should be able

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:07.020
to start those schools

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:08.650
but he could not

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:11.259
teach creationism as the--

00:08:11.259 --> 00:08:14.849
you know, he could not contradict the
curriculum.

00:08:14.849 --> 00:08:18.089
Now,

00:08:18.089 --> 00:08:21.789
you need, I think,

00:08:21.789 --> 00:08:22.580
to have certain--

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:24.639
to get this attitude of mind,

00:08:24.639 --> 00:08:29.839
I don't think you can leave it to chance--
I mean, this what Kenneth was saying--

00:08:29.839 --> 00:08:31.669
I 

00:08:31.669 --> 00:08:33.470
believe, for example, that

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:36.589
because of the importance-- the reason for 
this course and the

00:08:36.589 --> 00:08:38.780
reason why I think

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:42.840
education policy has got to take account of
the fact that religious and faith literacy

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:45.540
today is an important part of understanding the
society

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:49.050
and world in which you grow up, and therefore I think
that needs to amend,

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:50.140
you know,

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:52.729
the policy that we have.

00:08:52.729 --> 00:08:55.290
Now does that mean that everybody

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:56.890
will agree?

00:08:56.890 --> 00:09:02.340
No. And does that mean occasionally, as
with Switzerland, that you end up in a

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:03.820
situation where people

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:06.540
collectively take an action that is contrary

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:07.540
to the basic--

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:08.399
to, really, what 

00:09:08.399 --> 00:09:10.750
the country is about, actually.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:13.170
No, it doesn't mean that. You will get that.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:17.120
But that makes it all the more important

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:18.250
for that open attitude. 

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:20.800
to be, I think,

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:23.490
quite a deliberative

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:25.040
proposition advanced

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.120
on behalf of the--

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.390
WOLSTERSTORFF: Does Switzerland have a constitution?

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:35.090
BLAIR: It does have a constitution, but the constitution
tends to focus more on	

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:38.320
the sort of

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:40.380
technical, you know--

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:44.690
the division between the powers of the central
government and the cantons and so on. And although

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:45.260
it does have--

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:46.050
it does have

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:47.410
human rights legislation.

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:52.640
Now I think one thing that is interesting
is this, and this-- both Allen's point and Rich's 

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:53.670
points

00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:58.130
do this, I think:

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:02.700
that there will inevitably be situations in which
there's a conflict. Right?

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:06.750
And what's more, and I think this is a very important
point about the way things evolve--

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:08.470
I mean, I used to study

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:11.240
the American constitutional cases--

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:14.620
you know Brown and Board of Education and so on and
so forth and--

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:16.360
the fact is what happened

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:19.860
was that the courts, in a sense, reflected,

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:24.610
as their decisions changed, the evolution of
society.

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:27.230
And so certain attitudes 

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:32.090
held at one time became, over time, unacceptable
attitudes. Now, but here's what I think is important

00:10:32.090 --> 00:10:33.920
though:

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:38.400
the fact that you had a proper due
process,

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:40.080
and the fact that you have a

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:42.040
framework within which these

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:43.120
arguments

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:46.300
take place-- I think this is of itself helpful.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.770
And that it takes place

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:51.230
by reference to an argument

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:54.870
that is an argument that in some sense 
is, I think,

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:58.030
morally, not merely contractually, inspired. 

00:10:58.030 --> 00:10:59.490
Now I think, you know-- so

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:01.970
when I was

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:05.270
kind of your age as a student, I used to say,

00:11:05.270 --> 00:11:09.490
"Look, really, all that's happened is society's
changed."

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:13.580
But now I take a slightly more sophisticated
view of that process, and think,

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:16.400
"Yes, it's true, but actually the very fact

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:18.820
that there was a process within which these--

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:21.850
these issues were aired and determined

00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:26.820
that had reference to this constitutional framework
of rights was actually very important in resolving

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:27.420
this

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:31.450
in the correct way." And you might have waited,
incidentally, a lot longer

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:36.860
for some of these decisions to come, right?
Were it not for that fact. Now,

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:39.270
it is, however,

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:40.900
a situation

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:43.710
in which it does call for

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:45.130
open attitudes

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:46.290
from the non-religious

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:47.680
and the religious.

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:49.600
And where you get on--

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:54.060
where you get, for example, a closed attitude
on behalf of the religious,

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:56.010
you will get the reaction

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:57.990
from the non-religious.

00:11:57.990 --> 00:11:59.650
And that's why this debate--

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:06.060
it's quite important that it's-- it sees by
what I would say is the sort of sensible and intelligent

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:07.910
conceptual middle ground, to--

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:09.030
to push

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:12.890
the extremes back, because--

00:12:12.890 --> 00:12:15.370
let's be very clear about this.

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:20.380
The reaction in parts of Europe

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:22.790
that is taken on in certain

00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:24.470
occasions, an anti-

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:25.660
Islamic

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:27.340
character

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:29.150
is as a result of the feeling

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:30.870
that there are those within

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:34.860
the religious faith of islam who are pushing.

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:39.530
what people think is an inconsistent view
about basic politics on the rest of the population.

00:12:39.530 --> 00:12:41.059
So that's why you need--

00:12:41.059 --> 00:12:46.970
this is why I say, I think this debate--
'cause I really do believe, in a sense, that

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:50.170
for the majority of people, I mean, what Nick

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:51.270
has outlined is--

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:54.070
you know, I think as a

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:58.120
I mean, would kind of win the election,
as it were-- I mean,

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:02.380
you know, in other words-- most people would say,
"Well, it seems to me sensible enough,"

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:03.130
because it's--

00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:06.209
it's a pretty open attitude. But it has to
be

00:13:06.209 --> 00:13:10.150
something, then-- I think it's the policy
conclusion from that that interests me,

00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:11.509
this-- how you then

00:13:11.509 --> 00:13:13.430
can inculcate and encourage that,

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:18.500
how civic society, not just government,
takes a role in that-- how academic institutions,

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:19.690
also, develop

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:22.070
the research that shows that

00:13:22.070 --> 00:13:24.840
the open societies

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:26.850
can also lead to--

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:29.160
to greater

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:30.640
endeavour and prosperity.

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:32.339
You know, I was saying to the

00:13:32.339 --> 00:13:35.599
colleagues who were here from Peking University
today that one really interesting thing

00:13:35.599 --> 00:13:36.420
to me is,

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:38.830
in China, how

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:40.550
the modern leadership

00:13:40.550 --> 00:13:45.180
is referencing its

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:47.100
open

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:48.600
policy today,

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:50.130
not to the changes

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:51.779
of the sixty years since the

00:13:51.779 --> 00:13:55.950
the revolution in China, but actually
way back in time

00:13:55.950 --> 00:13:57.900
to, say, when

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:03.240
Confucianism was developing, and
China was at that point a very

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:06.120
open trading nation-- that was when it was successful.

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:08.800
It then closed down, became unsuccessful.

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:10.680
And now it's opening up again it--

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:12.260
you know, and-- I think

00:14:12.260 --> 00:14:14.750
these things have to be fought for; that's my
point.

00:14:14.750 --> 00:14:18.600
So when you look at the Netherlands--
I mean, most--

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:20.170
most people are--

00:14:20.170 --> 00:14:23.190
are obviously shocked at what happened.

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:24.990
But the only way of

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:26.490
retrieving the situation

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:29.120
is to try and give some sense of 

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:30.050
a common

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:30.870
space and

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:32.720
a common set of shared

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:34.069
purposes and values. So,

00:14:34.069 --> 00:14:38.560
for example, after the London bombing
in July

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:43.970
2005,

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:46.319
my first statement-- I mean, I knew you would get

00:14:46.319 --> 00:14:48.350
at a later stage

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:50.960
to other things, but my first statement

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:52.830
was a statement about

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:53.830
shared values,

00:14:53.830 --> 00:14:55.860
about the fact that

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:59.370
any anti-Muslim sentiment coming out of this
would be wrong and aggressive,

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:03.450
and would undermine the basis of our society.

00:15:03.450 --> 00:15:04.890
So that was my

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:09.550
first-- almost like a kind of philosophical
political statement to bring people together.

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:13.530
But I was also aware, and when I got to this
a few months later, people kind of thought one

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:15.210
was inconsistent with the other--

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:18.830
where I said, "Right, now we're also going to use
the following rules, 

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:20.169
right? We are not going to have

00:15:20.169 --> 00:15:22.060
imams coming in from abroad

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:25.060
who are not certified by some process

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:27.040
as having a

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:28.910
certain amount of religious

00:15:28.910 --> 00:15:30.469
achievement, and also a

00:15:30.469 --> 00:15:31.779
particular attitude."

00:15:31.779 --> 00:15:34.860
We can't afford to have someone coming into
our society

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:36.770
saying, "You are the enemy if you are not 

00:15:36.770 --> 00:15:38.340
of my religion."

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:40.020
So, and then people said, "Well that's--

00:15:40.020 --> 00:15:42.030
you know, there's this very nice

00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:45.670
broad philosophical statement, and now you're
taking some quite tough measures here."

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:47.740
But I said, you know, the one is necessary

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:49.920
in order to protect the other.

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:52.190
And actually what happens

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:53.920
is-- and I have this debate often with

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:55.230
people on the

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:58.590
progressive side of politics is that--

00:15:58.590 --> 00:16:00.260
such open attitudes

00:16:00.260 --> 00:16:03.010
and the protection of your basic

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:06.110
common space-- it doesn't happen by accident,

00:16:06.110 --> 00:16:07.150
right? And it--

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:09.780
there are plenty of people who will--

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:14.610
it might happen naturally, but there are also people
prepared to interfere with that process.

00:16:14.610 --> 00:16:16.019
And so you do have to--

00:16:16.019 --> 00:16:19.150
you know, the policy implications of this, I think,
are quite,

00:16:19.150 --> 00:16:20.089
quite profound,

00:16:20.089 --> 00:16:20.630
and make 

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:22.690
a lot of difference in the way that

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:25.370
politics then interacts with the world of
religion.

00:16:25.370 --> 00:16:28.200
VOLF: É this point with the Swiss case with the ban, 

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:33.370
if I'm informed correctly, it is the complacency
of the opponents of the ban

00:16:33.370 --> 00:16:38.540
that allowed the ban to go through. They simply
took it for granted that Swiss will not

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:40.510
pass the vote, right? So it's 

00:16:40.510 --> 00:16:45.190
a lock of democratic functioning-- in a sense,
democratic engagement that

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:48.780
allowed it to happen.
VOLTERSTORFF: And I think what happened in Dutch--

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:51.140
my ancestry is Dutch, so I

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:52.240
care about it and so forth--

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:57.410
so I think about what happened in the Dutch
case, and it's still happening,

00:16:57.410 --> 00:16:59.090
is that Holland had been-- had become

00:16:59.090 --> 00:17:02.180
a very homogeneous society; it 

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:05.069
for centuries had had a Jewish component, but it--

00:17:05.069 --> 00:17:08.179
it worked out for accommodations and

00:17:08.179 --> 00:17:11.340
Jews held honored positions in the society and so forth--

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:12.499
and now suddenly--

00:17:12.499 --> 00:17:15.720
and the Dutch are feeling good about themselves and all that--

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:18.480
now suddenly there's this group

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:21.910
who in all kinds of ways is simply not
part of the Dutch consensus. They've got no interest

00:17:21.910 --> 00:17:26.990
in participating in the Dutch government. They do get

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:31.070
"doles" from the Dutch government, and this is
resented-- welfare from the Dutch

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:35.059
government, and this is resented and so forth.

00:17:35.059 --> 00:17:35.870
So--

00:17:35.870 --> 00:17:39.270
I think American society and probably British
society would have been--

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:40.370
would be far

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:42.420
better able to handle

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:46.870
this new group than the Dutch were. They
had no experience with--

00:17:46.870 --> 00:17:48.850
or such experience that they had was

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:50.330
centuries in the past. 

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:53.309
This was outside

00:17:53.309 --> 00:17:58.289
the implicit consensus they'd had--
BLAIR: I'd emend that by saying, I actually think America would have 
handled this better then

00:17:58.289 --> 00:17:59.250
either of our--

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:00.659
WOLTERSTORFF: Ok.
BLAIR: I think

00:18:00.659 --> 00:18:02.430
European society--

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:04.460
I think your very

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:09.640
constitution, with the points that you're making, is one of the major
reasons why.
WOLTERSTORFF: And we're much bigger-- I mean,

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:14.500
fifteen million homogenous people--
suddenly you-- 

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:15.920
as opposed to how many do--

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:17.429
three hundred million, whatever?

00:18:17.429 --> 00:18:18.000
Not homogenous. 

